# Linux Brewfile.
#
# This deliberately does NOT mirror macos/brew/Brewfile. On macOS, Homebrew is
# how you get GNU userland at all, because the system ships BSD tools. On Linux
# the distro already provides GNU, so installing the same formulae here does not
# upgrade anything, it shadows the system copy and drags in a parallel stack.
#
# Two concrete failures caused by doing that:
#
#   pkgconf   Homebrew's pkg-config searches only Homebrew prefixes. Every
#             system library probe returns "not found" no matter what is
#             installed, which read as missing GTK development headers and
#             parked real work behind a nonexistent problem.
#
#   openjdk   Unused here, but it pulls in fontconfig, glib, cairo, harfbuzz,
#             the X libraries, dbus and util-linux. That util-linux then
#             shadows mount, umount, nsenter, unshare and findmnt with a build
#             whose libmount has no verity support, which is exactly the kind
#             of mount option that fails open and silently does nothing.
#
# Rule for this file: add a formula only when the distro does not ship it, or
# ships a version too old to use. Anything that duplicates the system copy
# belongs in macos/brew/Brewfile only.
#
# coreutils is the deliberate exception. Ubuntu now ships uutils coreutils,
# which is not fully GNU compatible, so Homebrew's GNU build is a real upgrade
# rather than a duplicate.

